<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/foundational/worlds-traditions">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Studying religion academically is not believing, debunking or preaching — it is asking how
    traditions actually work: what they claim, how they organise communities, what rituals do, and how
    texts get interpreted. That distinction is harder to hold than it sounds, because religion is the
    one subject where nearly everyone arrives with a settled position. Religious studies therefore
    begins with method, not content. Two ground rules protect the study. First, <strong>traditions are
    plural and changing</strong> — "Hinduism" or "Islam" name vast, internally diverse families, not
    single fixed things; the word "Hinduism" itself is largely a 19th-century umbrella term for what
    had been many distinct traditions, and Christianity broke into Catholic, Orthodox and thousands of
    Protestant branches. Second, <strong>believers are the experts on their own experience</strong>;
    the scholar's job is comparison and history, not verdicts on truth.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="founding moments and turning points across three continents" widget="timeline" config={relTimeline} />

  <h2>Studying religion academically: emic, etic, and the method of comparison</h2>
  <p>
    The field's central methodological pair comes from linguistics (Kenneth Pike, 1954). The
    <strong>emic</strong> perspective is the insider's: describing a practice in the categories that
    matter to participants — what the fast <em>means</em> to the faster. The <strong>etic</strong>
    perspective is the outsider's analytical frame: describing the same practice in comparative,
    scholarly categories — what the fast <em>does</em> socially, psychologically, historically. Good
    scholarship needs both and keeps them visibly separate: an account that reports only etic
    generalisations can end up explaining the tradition in terms its adherents wouldn't recognise; an
    account that reports only emic testimony cannot compare or explain. Comparison — the discipline's
    engine — asks across traditions: what work does this ritual do? How is authority organised? What
    is the diagnosis of the human problem? The most used comparative scaffold is Ninian Smart's
    <strong>seven dimensions</strong> of religion: ritual, narrative and mythic, experiential and
    emotional, social and institutional, ethical and legal, doctrinal and philosophical, and material
    (buildings, art, places). The dimensions do not define religion — they give the comparativist a
    checklist of <em>where to look</em>, and they make visible why traditions differ: Buddhism is thin
    on doctrine about a creator god and thick on practice; some traditions privilege the institution,
    others the inner experience.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "myth" in this field does not mean "false story". It means the
  <em>foundational narratives a people lives by</em> — creation, flood, hero's journey, founding
  law-giver. Myths encode how societies understood nature, justice and mortality; reading them
  comparatively is reading humanity's oldest self-portraits. Calling a tradition's narratives "myths"
  is a technical term, not a verdict — and a careful scholar flags which meaning is in play.</p>

  <h2>The questions every tradition answers</h2>
  <p>
    Comparisons get traction because traditions answer a shared question set, even when the answers
    diverge. <strong>Ultimate reality</strong>: one God, many gods, an impersonal absolute
    (Brahman), or no god at all (Buddhism's non-theistic path) — the answer organises everything
    downstream. <strong>The human problem</strong>: sin (needing salvation), ignorance (needing
    awakening), suffering (needing release), disharmony (needing balance) — each diagnosis generates
    its own prescription, which is why practices differ more than vocabulary suggests.
    <strong>Authority</strong>: scripture, priesthood, direct experience, or lineage — who may
    interpret is often the real fault line (see the 1054 schism, and the Reformation's scriptura sola).
    <strong>Ritual</strong>: shared meals, prayer, pilgrimage, initiations — religion is performed as
    much as believed, and the performance (Smart's ritual and material dimensions) is what
    anthropology and sociology study most closely. With that scaffold, the major traditions become
    readable as systems rather than lists of facts.
  </p>

  <h2>Hinduism: Vedas to bhakti</h2>
  <p>
    The oldest continuous family of traditions on the timeline. Its roots are the <strong>Vedas</strong>
    — hymn collections composed in Sanskrit c. 1500–900 BCE, preserved for centuries by exact oral
    recitation before writing — which carry the ritual world of sacrifice to powers like Agni and
    Indra. Between roughly 800 and 300 BCE the <strong>Upanishads</strong> turned the tradition
    inward and philosophical: the inner self (<em>ātman</em>) explored until it meets ultimate reality
    (<em>Brahman</em>), with liberation (<em>moksha</em>) as release from the cycle of rebirth
    (<em>saṃsāra</em>) — driven by karma, the law that actions bear fruit. On top of this philosophy
    grew the <em>dharma</em> framework: duty, law and right order, elaborated in texts on conduct and
    in the great epics (<em>Mahābhārata</em>, <em>Rāmāyaṇa</em>), with the <em>Bhagavad Gītā</em>
    teaching that one's own duty, done without attachment to results, is itself a spiritual path.
    And from around the 6th century CE, the <strong>bhakti movement</strong> — passionate devotional
    love toward a personal god, whether Viṣṇu, Śiva or the Goddess — swept from south Indian poet-
    saints across the subcontinent, democratising religion: devotion and song became paths open to
    those excluded from Sanskrit ritual learning. Modern Hinduism is the still-evolving sum of all
    these layers — Vedic ritual, Upanishadic philosophy, epic ethics, bhakti devotion — which is
    precisely why scholars describe it as a family of traditions rather than one religion: some
    practitioners are devoted monotheists, some worship many gods as manifestations of one, some
    orient toward the impersonal absolute. All count.
  </p>

  <h2>Buddhism: four truths, two vehicles, many cultures</h2>
  <p>
    Emerging in the same century as Jainism's rigorous non-violence, Buddhism starts from a
    diagnosis. The <strong>Four Noble Truths</strong>: (1) existence involves <em>dukkha</em> —
    suffering, unsatisfactoriness, the friction of wanting things to hold still; (2) it arises from
    <em>tṛṣṇā</em>, craving and clinging; (3) it can cease; and (4) the path to that cessation is the
    <strong>noble eightfold path</strong> — a training in view, intention, speech, action, livelihood,
    effort, mindfulness and concentration, ethics and meditation braided together. Crucially,
    Buddhism is <strong>non-theistic</strong>: the Buddha is not a creator god but a teacher, and the
    tradition's engine is his claim that craving can be trained out — verifiable in one's own
    experience. The tradition divided over how and for whom that training works. <strong>Theravada</strong>
    ("the teaching of the elders") — dominant in Sri Lanka and Southeast Asia — preserves the
    earliest texts and holds up the <em>arhat</em> ideal: the individual who attains liberation
    through disciplined monastic practice. <strong>Mahayana</strong> ("the great vehicle") — across
    China, Korea, Japan, Vietnam — re-centres the <em>bodhisattva</em>: one who vows to delay final
    nirvana until all beings can cross, making compassion structurally primary and opening the door
    to new scriptures and devotional forms (Zen's direct experience, Pure Land's reliance on
    Amitābha). Vajrayana's tantric paths add a third current (Tibet, Bhutan, Mongolia). "Two
    vehicles, many cultures" is the honest summary: the four truths travel, and every culture that
    received them — from Sri Lankan forest monasteries to Japanese tea rooms to Western meditation
    apps — rewrote the packaging.
  </p>

  <h2>The Abrahamic line and Sikhism</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Judaism</strong> (Merneptah's stele, c. 1208 BCE, is the earliest archaeological mention of
    Israel) is the root of the Abrahamic family: covenant — a binding relationship between one God and
    a people — with Torah, law and rabbinic interpretation as the machinery of continuity across two
    millennia without a homeland. <strong>Christianity</strong> emerged from that world around Jesus of
    Nazareth (executed c. 30 CE); his followers' resurrection claim founded a movement that by 380 had
    become the Roman empire's official religion — an institutional triumph that immediately raised the
    authority questions that split it (1054: Catholic West and Orthodox East; 1517: the Reformation).
    <strong>Islam</strong> (Muhammad's revelations from c. 610; the hijra to Medina in 622 begins the
    Islamic calendar) teaches radical unity of God (<em>tawḥīd</em>) with the Qur'an as final
    revelation; within a century it reached Spain and the Indus, and its own great division — Sunni
    and Shi'a, over succession to Muhammad — is the timeline's other lesson in plurality.
    <strong>Sikhism</strong>, founded by Guru Nanak in Punjab (1469–1539), is the youngest of the
    five: a synthesis of devotional poetry and rigorous divine unity, with equality before God made
    practical — the <em>langar</em>, a free shared meal served to everyone regardless of caste, creed
    or wealth, is the teaching in edible form — and authority vested in the scripture itself (the
    Guru Granth Sahib) after ten human Gurus.
  </p>

  <h2>Philosophy of religion: arguments for and against, charitably staged</h2>
  <p>
    Finally, the question the academic study cannot duck without dishonesty: what can be <em>argued</em>
    about religious claims? The classical <strong>arguments for</strong> God's existence each start
    somewhere different. The <strong>cosmological argument</strong> starts from existence itself:
    everything that begins to exist has a cause; the universe begins to exist; therefore a first cause
    outside the chain (Aquinas's "five ways"; the Islamic <em>kalām</em> version of al-Ghazali).
    The <strong>teleological (design) argument</strong> starts from order: Paley's watch —
    a mechanism found on a heath implies a watchmaker; nature is more mechanism still. The
    <strong>ontological argument</strong> starts from the concept alone: Anselm — God is "that than
    which nothing greater can be conceived"; existence in reality is greater than existence in the
    mind alone; therefore God exists (Kant's objection: existence is not a predicate that can be added
    to a concept). The <strong>arguments against</strong> are just as developed: the <strong>problem of
    evil</strong> — Mackie's inconsistent triad: God is omnipotent, wholly good, yet evil exists; the
    classical replies (free will, soul-making) answer the logical problem but strain against the
    <em>scale</em> of suffering; Hume's critique of <strong>miracle testimony</strong> — it is always
    more likely that witnesses err than that nature's uniformity breaks; and the naturalistic
    explanations of religious experience and belief from psychology and sociology.
    "Charitably staged" is the methodological rule that makes this productive rather than tribal:
    argue against the <em>strongest</em> version of the opposing case (the principle of charity), and
    notice that the arguments assess philosophy, not the lived tradition — a believer and a philosopher
    of religion are doing different things, and the honest scholar marks the boundary.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three currents run beneath the survey. <strong>Definitions are arguments:</strong> decades of
    scholarship (Durkheim's social functionalism — religion as society worshipping itself; Marx's
    critique — the "opium of the people"; Freud's projection theory; Geertz's anthropological
    definition of religion as a symbol system; recent critics like Timothy Fitzgerald arguing the
    category "religion" itself is a Western Protestant construction projected onto the world) show
    that <em>what counts as religion</em> is a live analytical choice with consequences — Buddhism has
    no god, Confucianism has no church, and any definition draws boundaries someone contests.
    <strong>Plurality is internal:</strong> every tradition on the timeline is a family — Hinduism's
    layered history above, Buddhism's vehicles, Christianity's schisms, Islam's Sunni-Shi'a divide,
    Judaism's Orthodox/Reform spectrum, plus the newer global movements (Baha'i, Mormonism, Pentecostal
    growth in the Global South) — so the comparative method's first finding is always variance.
    <strong>The academic study changed the traditions too:</strong> the 1893 World's Parliament of
    Religions in Chicago (Vivekananda's address made Hinduism, for the first time, a world
    interfaith participant) marks the birth of comparative religion as public practice — and of the
    modern habit, now universal, of traditions presenting themselves comparatively. Scrub the timeline
    once more with that in mind and the pattern holds from the Vedas to Vivekananda: traditions are
    not fossils of founding moments but ongoing arguments about meaning, conducted in ritual and text
    and institution — which is exactly why studying them academically, emic and etic together, is
    the only approach that lets the arguments be heard.
  </p>
